-- ==============================================================================
-- RECTIFIED PRACTICE HUB COMPLETE MCQ CONTENT MIGRATION
-- Ensures every practice problem has complete 4-option MCQ, explanation,
-- hint, constraints, example, and +20 XP reward.
-- ==============================================================================

-- 1. Add missing columns to public.practice_problems if not present
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS example_input TEXT;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS example_output TEXT;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS explanation TEXT;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS constraints TEXT;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS hint TEXT;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS options JSONB DEFAULT '[]'::jsonb;
ALTER TABLE public.practice_problems ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS correct_index INTEGER DEFAULT 0;

-- 2. Ensure table grants for anon and authenticated
GRANT SELECT ON public.practice_problems TO anon, authenticated;
GRANT SELECT ON public.quiz_questions TO anon, authenticated;
GRANT SELECT ON public.interview_questions TO anon, authenticated;

-- 3. Seed / Update all 52 Practice Problems with complete MCQ content
INSERT INTO public.practice_problems (
    id, title, category, difficulty, description, career_id, topic,
    xp_reward, example_input, example_output, explanation, constraints,
    hint, options, correct_index
) VALUES
('dsa_01', 'Two Sum & Hash Map Lookup', 'Coding', 'Easy', 'You are given an integer array nums and an integer target. Your task is to find the indices of the two distinct elements whose values add up to target. You may assume that each input has exactly one valid pair of numbers, and you cannot use the same element twice.

For nums = [2, 7, 11, 15] and target = 9, which algorithmic technique correctly solves the problem in optimal O(N) time complexity and O(N) auxiliary space?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'software_dev', 'Arrays & Hashing', 20, 'nums = [2, 7, 11, 15], target = 9', 'Index pair [i, j] such that nums[i] + nums[j] == target', 'Iterating once through nums while storing visited elements and their indices in a Hash Map allows complement lookups (target - nums[i]) in O(1) average time. At index 1 (value 7), complement 9 - 7 = 2 is found at index 0, yielding [0, 1] in O(N) total time.', '2 <= nums.length <= 10^4, -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9. Exactly one valid pair exists.', 'As you iterate through the array, calculate complement = target - nums[i] and check if that complement already exists in your hash map.', '["Single pass with a Hash Map storing visited values and indices to look up complements in O(N) time","Brute-force nested loops checking all possible index pairs (i, j) in O(N^2) time","Sort the array in O(N log N) time and use binary search to locate original indices","Recursive backtracking generating all 2^N subsets until a sum matches target"]'::jsonb, 0),
('dsa_02', 'Reverse a Singly Linked List', 'Coding', 'Easy', 'You are given the head of a singly linked list containing sequential values 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> NULL. You need to reverse the direction of all pointer links in place so that the list becomes 5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> NULL and return the new head node.

Which pointer manipulation technique achieves this in-place reversal in O(N) linear time using strictly O(1) auxiliary memory?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'software_dev', 'Linked Lists', 20, 'head = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]', 'Reversed list structure with pointers directed toward predecessors', 'By maintaining three pointers (prev, curr, nextNode), we iteratively save curr.next, redirect curr.next to prev, advance prev to curr, and advance curr to nextNode. When curr reaches NULL, prev points to the new head (node 5), taking O(N) time and O(1) extra space.', 'Number of nodes in list is in the range [0, 5000]. In-place pointer modifications only.', 'Maintain three pointer references: prev (initialized to null), curr (initialized to head), and a temporary nextNode to save curr.next before redirecting curr.next = prev.', '["Copy all node values into an auxiliary array, reverse the array, and overwrite node values","Iterate through nodes using three pointers (prev, curr, next) to reverse curr.next pointers in O(1) space","Allocate a brand new linked list and prepend copies of every node in reverse order","Swap values between pairs of nodes recursively without modifying node next references"]'::jsonb, 1),
('dsa_03', 'Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters', 'Coding', 'Medium', 'Given a string s = "abcabcbb", you need to find the length of the longest contiguous substring that contains no duplicate characters. For example, in "abcabcbb", the longest non-repeating substrings are "abc", "bca", and "cab", each with a length of 3.

Which algorithmic strategy finds the maximum length of a non-repeating substring in optimal O(N) time complexity?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'software_dev', 'Sliding Window', 20, 's = "abcabcbb"', 'Length of longest contiguous substring containing unique characters', 'A sliding window bounded by left and right pointers tracks the active substring. A Hash Map stores each character''s last observed index. When character s[right] has been seen inside the current window, left is updated to max(left, lastSeen[s[right]] + 1). This processes each character at most twice, achieving O(N) time and O(min(N, AlphabetSize)) space.', '0 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^4. s consists of English letters, digits, symbols, and spaces.', 'Maintain a dynamic window [left, right] with a Hash Set or Hash Map storing the most recent index of each character. When a duplicate is encountered at right, advance the left pointer past its previous occurrence.', '["Generate all O(N^2) possible substrings and verify character uniqueness for each in O(N^3) total time","Sort all characters in the string alphabetically and count the longest continuous non-duplicate block","Use a sliding window with two pointers and a Hash Map/Set tracking character positions in O(N) time","Count character global frequencies with a single integer array and subtract duplicate counts"]'::jsonb, 2),
('dsa_04', 'Binary Tree Level Order Traversal', 'Coding', 'Medium', 'You are given the root of a binary tree: [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]. You are required to traverse the tree level by level from top to bottom, grouping node values horizontally from left to right for each depth level, producing [[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]].

Which data structure and traversal pattern guarantees that all nodes at depth d are processed before any nodes at depth d+1?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'software_dev', 'Trees & Graphs', 20, 'root = [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]', 'Nested list containing node values grouped by tree depth', 'Breadth-First Search (BFS) using a FIFO queue naturally processes nodes level by level. By recording levelSize = queue.size() before processing each level, we dequeue all levelSize nodes into a sublist while enqueuing their children for the next level. This runs in O(N) time and O(W) space where W is the maximum tree width.', 'The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [0, 2000]. -1000 <= Node.val <= 1000.', 'Use a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) Queue. At the start of each level loop, capture the current queue size, poll that exact number of nodes for the level list, and enqueue their non-null left and right children.', '["Depth-First Search using a LIFO Stack to traverse all left descendants before right descendants","In-Order Traversal recording node values into a sorted Red-Black balanced binary tree","Topological sort on node memory pointers using a priority queue","Breadth-First Search using a FIFO Queue and recording queue size at each level boundary in O(N) time"]'::jsonb, 3),
('dsa_05', 'Climbing Stairs (Dynamic Programming)', 'Coding', 'Easy', 'You are climbing a staircase that has n steps to reach the top. Each time you take a step, you can climb either 1 step or 2 steps. Given n = 3 steps, there are 3 distinct ways to reach the top: (1+1+1), (1+2), and (2+1).

Which dynamic programming recurrence relation and base cases correctly compute the total number of distinct ways to climb n stairs in O(n) time and O(1) space?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'software_dev', 'Dynamic Programming', 20, 'n = 4', 'Total count of distinct step combinations reaching step n', 'The number of ways to reach step n is the sum of ways to reach step n-1 and step n-2: dp[n] = dp[n-1] + dp[n-2]. Base cases are dp[1] = 1 and dp[2] = 2. This represents the Fibonacci sequence and can be computed iteratively in O(n) time and O(1) space by tracking only the two previous values.', '1 <= n <= 45. The answer fits within a standard 32-bit signed integer.', 'To arrive at step i, you could have only jumped from step i-1 (by taking 1 step) or from step i-2 (by taking 2 steps).', '["dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] with base cases dp[1] = 1, dp[2] = 2","dp[i] = dp[i-1] * dp[i-2] with base cases dp[1] = 1, dp[2] = 2","dp[i] = 2 * dp[i-1] - 1 with base case dp[1] = 1","Exhaustive unmemoized recursion branches yielding O(2^n) exponential complexity"]'::jsonb, 0),
('aiml_01', 'Vector Dot Product & Cosine Similarity', 'Coding', 'Easy', 'A semantic search engine encodes document and query text into dense embedding vectors A and B of dimension D = 1536. To rank document relevance independent of vector magnitudes, the system computes the cosine of the angle between vectors A and B.

Which mathematical formula represents the Cosine Similarity between vector A and vector B?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'aiml_engineer', 'Linear Algebra & Embeddings', 20, 'A = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0], B = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]', 'Scalar similarity score in range [-1.0, 1.0]', 'Cosine Similarity is defined as cos(theta) = (A · B) / (||A||_2 * ||B||_2) = (sum(A[i]*B[i])) / (sqrt(sum(A[i]^2)) * sqrt(sum(B[i]^2))). It measures orientation rather than magnitude, bounded in [-1.0, 1.0].', 'Vectors A and B are non-zero vectors in R^D where 1 <= D <= 1536.', 'Cosine similarity measures the normalized inner product: the dot product of the vectors divided by the product of their Euclidean L2 norms.', '["Euclidean Distance: sqrt(sum((A[i] - B[i])^2))","Normalized Dot Product: (A · B) / (||A||_2 * ||B||_2)","Manhattan L1 Distance: sum(|A[i] - B[i]|)","Matrix Determinant: det(A (x) B^T)"]'::jsonb, 1),
('aiml_02', 'Cross-Entropy Loss & Softmax Computation', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A neural network classifier outputs uncalibrated real-valued logits z = [2.0, 1.0, 0.1] for C = 3 mutually exclusive classes. The model needs to convert these logits into a valid probability distribution p and compute the loss for a ground-truth one-hot label y where class 0 is the true target (y = [1, 0, 0]).

Which combination of activation function and loss function is the industry standard for multi-class classification, and why?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'aiml_engineer', 'Deep Learning Fundamentals', 20, 'Classification with C classes, unnormalized output logits z = [z_1, ..., z_C]', 'Normalized class probability vector and scalar cross-entropy loss', 'Softmax converts logits into probabilities via p_i = exp(z_i) / sum(exp(z_j)). Categorical Cross-Entropy loss L = -sum(y_i * log(p_i)) penalizes incorrect predictions logarithmically, maximizing the log-likelihood of the ground-truth class. Its gradient (p - y) is simple and numerically stable during backpropagation.', 'Number of classes C >= 2. Probability distribution must sum to 1.0.', 'Softmax normalizes exponential logits into probabilities that sum to 1. Categorical Cross-Entropy takes the negative natural logarithm of the predicted probability for the true class.', '["Sigmoid activation on each output node with Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss","ReLU activation across output nodes with Hinge Loss","Softmax activation to generate a probability distribution with Categorical Cross-Entropy loss","Softmax activation with Mean Absolute Error (MAE) loss"]'::jsonb, 2),
('aiml_03', 'K-Means Clustering Convergence', 'Coding', 'Medium', 'You are implementing the standard Lloyd''s algorithm for K-Means clustering on an unlabeled dataset of N feature vectors with K target cluster centroids. In each iteration, points are assigned to the nearest centroid and centroids are updated.

Which mathematical objective function does K-Means iteratively minimize to guarantee convergence to a local minimum?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'aiml_engineer', 'Unsupervised Learning', 20, 'K = 3 clusters, N = 1000 2D feature points, convergence threshold epsilon = 1e-4', 'Partitioned clusters minimizing intra-cluster centroid variance', 'Lloyd''s K-Means alternates between the Expectation step (assigning each point to its closest centroid) and the Maximization step (recomputing centroids as the arithmetic mean of assigned points). Each step monotonically decreases the Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (WCSS = sum_k sum_{x in C_k} ||x - mu_k||^2), guaranteeing convergence.', 'K >= 1, Euclidean distance metric used for point-centroid assignment.', 'K-Means minimizes the Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (WCSS), which measures the sum of squared Euclidean distances between points and their assigned cluster centroid.', '["Maximizing between-cluster variance using gradient ascent on silhouette coefficients","Minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence between sample clusters and Gaussian priors","Maximizing the convex hull area enclosing each of the K cluster boundaries","Minimizing Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (WCSS) through alternating assignment and mean recalculation"]'::jsonb, 3),
('aiml_04', 'Convolution Output Dimensions & Weight Sizing', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'A 2D convolutional layer receives an input image volume of dimensions 32 x 32 x 3 (Height x Width x Channels). The layer applies 16 filters, each of size 3 x 3, with a stride of S = 1 and zero padding of P = 0.

What are the spatial height, spatial width, and channel depth dimensions (H_out x W_out x C_out) of the resulting output feature map?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'aiml_engineer', 'Computer Vision & CNNs', 20, 'Input tensor: 32x32x3, Filters: 16 kernels of size 3x3, Stride: 1, Padding: 0', 'Spatial dimensions (Height x Width x Channels) of output feature tensor', 'Applying the formula: Height_out = floor((32 - 3 + 2*0) / 1) + 1 = 29 + 1 = 30. Width_out = floor((32 - 3 + 2*0) / 1) + 1 = 30. The number of filters is 16, so the output depth C_out = 16. The final output tensor shape is 30 x 30 x 16.', 'Standard 2D convolution arithmetic without dilation (Dilation = 1).', 'Use the spatial dimension formula: Output = floor((W - K + 2*P) / S) + 1. The output channel depth always equals the number of convolutional filters applied.', '["30 x 30 x 16","32 x 32 x 16","28 x 28 x 3","16 x 16 x 32"]'::jsonb, 0),
('aiml_05', 'Precision-Recall Curve & Threshold Optimization', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A machine learning model is deployed in a hospital to detect a rare malignant disease present in only 0.5% of tested patients. Failing to detect a true positive patient (a False Negative) has severe health consequences, while a False Positive results in a harmless follow-up screening.

Which evaluation metric must the engineering team prioritize and maximize when tuning the classification decision threshold?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'aiml_engineer', 'Model Evaluation', 20, 'Binary disease screening dataset with 0.5% positive prevalence and high misdiagnosis risk', 'Optimal metric selection for critical False Negative reduction', 'Recall (also called Sensitivity) measures the fraction of actual positive cases that were correctly identified by the model: Recall = TP / (TP + FN). When missing a positive case (False Negative) is dangerous or costly, the classification threshold is lowered to maximize Recall.', 'Severe class imbalance: Positive class prevalence = 0.5%. High cost for False Negatives.', 'Recall = True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives). High recall minimizes False Negatives.', '["Precision exclusively, in order to avoid issuing any false positive alarms","Recall (Sensitivity), in order to minimize False Negatives and capture virtually all disease cases","Raw Overall Accuracy, since a dummy model predicting all negatives achieves 99.5% accuracy","Specificity exclusively, to maximize true negative identification"]'::jsonb, 1),
('data_03', 'Hypothesis Testing & A/B Test Power Calculation', 'Technical', 'Hard', 'A data science team is designing an online A/B experiment to evaluate a new checkout workflow. The baseline conversion rate is p = 5.0%, and the team wants to detect a minimum detectable effect (MDE) of +1.0% (to 6.0%) with a significance level of alpha = 0.05 (Type I error) and a statistical power of 1 - beta = 0.80.

In statistical hypothesis testing, what does the statistical power (1 - beta = 0.80) specifically represent?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_scientist', 'Inferential Statistics', 20, 'A/B test design parameters: alpha = 0.05, beta = 0.20, statistical power = 1 - beta = 0.80', 'Statistical interpretation of experimental power in hypothesis testing', 'Statistical power (1 - beta) is the probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when an actual difference or effect exists. A power of 0.80 means the A/B test has an 80% chance of successfully detecting the 1.0% conversion uplift without committing a Type II false-negative error.', 'Two-tailed two-sample proportion Z-test under standard normal distribution assumptions.', 'Beta is the probability of committing a Type II error (failing to reject a false null hypothesis). Therefore, 1 - beta is the probability of correctly detecting a genuine effect.', '["The probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when the null hypothesis is actually true (Type I error)","The percentage of users who must be assigned to the control group during the experiment","The probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when an effect truly exists (1 - Type II error)","The proportion of variance explained by random demographic noise"]'::jsonb, 2),
('ds_01', 'Linear Regression: Normal Equation vs Gradient Descent', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'You need to train a multiple linear regression model on a dataset containing N = 500,000 training examples and D = 25,000 continuous features. You must choose between the analytical closed-form Normal Equation (theta = (X^T X)^(-1) X^T y) and iterative Batch Gradient Descent.

Why is iterative Gradient Descent strongly preferred over the analytical Normal Equation for this high-dimensional dataset?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_scientist', 'Statistical Modeling', 20, 'Tabular dataset dimensions: N = 500,000 samples, D = 25,000 continuous features', 'Computational complexity trade-off between analytical and iterative methods', 'The Normal Equation requires computing (X^T X)^(-1), which involves inverting a D x D matrix with computational complexity of O(D^3). For D = 25,000, inverting this matrix is computationally prohibitive and memory-intensive. In contrast, Gradient Descent scales efficiently with O(k * N * D) complexity.', 'Feature count D = 25,000. Matrix inversion computational complexity scales with feature count.', 'Consider the computational cost and memory footprint of calculating the inverse of the (D x D) matrix (X^T X).', '["The Normal Equation cannot find the global minimum for linear regression models","Gradient Descent does not require setting a learning rate hyperparameter","The Normal Equation requires all features to follow a standard Gaussian distribution","The Normal Equation requires inverting a D x D matrix in O(D^3) time, which is prohibitive for D = 25,000"]'::jsonb, 3),
('ds_02', 'Principal Component Analysis (PCA) & Variance Ratio', 'Coding', 'Medium', 'You have a dataset with 50 correlated numerical features. To reduce dimensionality while retaining at least 90% of the total dataset variance, you perform Principal Component Analysis (PCA) by computing the eigendecomposition of the sample covariance matrix Sigma = (1/N) * X_centered^T X_centered.

How does PCA construct these new orthogonal principal components and determine their individual importance?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_scientist', 'Dimensionality Reduction', 20, 'Mean-centered data matrix X with 50 continuous features across N samples', 'Orthogonal projection axes and explained variance proportions', 'PCA computes the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of the feature covariance matrix. The eigenvectors define mutually orthogonal directions of maximum variance, and each eigenvalue corresponds to the variance captured by that component. Sorting eigenvalues in descending order lets us select the top k components that exceed the target cumulative variance ratio.', 'Features must be mean-centered (and standardized if on different scales) prior to PCA.', 'The eigenvectors of the covariance matrix represent the orthogonal directions of maximum variance, while their corresponding eigenvalues indicate the amount of variance along each axis.', '["PCA computes orthogonal eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, with eigenvalues quantifying explained variance","PCA selects a subset of the original features without applying any linear transformations","PCA trains an iterative decision tree ensemble to score non-linear feature interactions","PCA maximizes class separation using supervised target label feedback"]'::jsonb, 0),
('ds_03', 'Feature Scaling: Standardization vs Min-Max Normalization', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'You are preparing a machine learning dataset containing numerical features with varying units and scales. You partition the data into an 80% training set and a 20% test set. You want to apply StandardScaler (z = (x - mu) / sigma) to ensure gradient-based models converge smoothly without introducing data leakage.

Which workflow correctly applies feature scaling without leaking information from the test set into the model training pipeline?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_scientist', 'Feature Engineering', 20, 'Partitioned dataset: X_train (80%), X_test (20%) requiring feature standardization', 'Safe parameter fitting and transformation pipeline without data leakage', 'To prevent data leakage, scaler statistics (mean mu and standard deviation sigma) must be calculated strictly from X_train using scaler.fit_transform(X_train). The exact same learned parameters are then used to transform X_test using scaler.transform(X_test). Fitting the scaler on the combined dataset or test set leaks distribution knowledge.', 'Strict prevention of data leakage between training and testing evaluation partitions.', 'The parameters (mean mu and standard deviation sigma) must be learned exclusively from the training partition and then applied to transform both datasets.', '["Fit the scaler parameters on the entire combined dataset before performing the train-test split","Fit the scaler exclusively on X_train, then use those learned parameters to transform both X_train and X_test","Fit two separate scalers independently: one on X_train and one on X_test","Scale the target label y while leaving input feature columns completely unstandardized"]'::jsonb, 1),
('ds_04', 'Random Forest Out-of-Bag (OOB) Error & Feature Importance', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'When training a Random Forest classifier with bootstrap aggregating (bagging) on N training samples, each individual decision tree is trained on a bootstrap sample drawn uniformly with replacement from the N instances.

Approximately what percentage of the original training instances are omitted from a given tree''s training set (known as Out-of-Bag samples), and how are they used?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_scientist', 'Ensemble Methods', 20, 'Training dataset of size N sampled with replacement for each ensemble tree', 'Proportion of omitted instances and their validation utility', 'In bootstrap sampling with replacement, the probability of an instance not being chosen in N draws is (1 - 1/N)^N. As N grows, this limit is 1/e ≈ 36.8%. These omitted ''Out-of-Bag'' (OOB) samples act as an internal, unbiased validation set for each tree, enabling computation of OOB error without a separate validation holdout.', 'Bootstrap sampling drawn uniformly with replacement: P(sample not chosen) = (1 - 1/N)^N -> 1/e.', 'As N approaches infinity, the probability of a sample not being selected in N draws with replacement converges to 1/e ≈ 0.368.', '["Exactly 50.0% of samples are omitted, which are discarded to reduce model memory usage","Exactly 10.0% of samples are omitted, used exclusively to compute tree depth bounds","Approximately 36.8% (~37%) of samples are omitted, serving as an internal validation set to estimate generalization error","0% of samples are omitted because bootstrap sampling always uses all instances"]'::jsonb, 2),
('data_01', 'SQL Window Functions: Top N Sales per Region', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'You are given a sales database table sales_records with columns (rep_id, rep_name, region, revenue). Your manager asks for a report showing the top 3 highest-earning sales representatives in each geographic region. If two representatives in the same region have identical revenue, both should receive the same rank without skipping subsequent rank numbers.

Which SQL window function clause correctly calculates this regional rank?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_analyst', 'SQL & Query Optimization', 20, 'Database schema: sales_records(rep_id, rep_name, region, revenue)', 'Window partition and ranking expression satisfying the gap-free ranking rule', 'DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY revenue DESC) evaluates ranks within each partition (region) ordered by revenue descending. Unlike RANK() which leaves gaps after ties (1, 2, 2, 4), DENSE_RANK() produces consecutive integers (1, 2, 2, 3), ensuring top-3 filtering (WHERE rnk <= 3) functions reliably.', 'Standard ANSI SQL: PostgreSQL, BigQuery, Snowflake, and MySQL 8.0+ compliant.', 'PARTITION BY creates separate ranking groups for each region. DENSE_RANK() assigns consecutive integer ranks without gaps when values tie.', '["ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY revenue DESC) GROUP BY region","RANK() OVER (ORDER BY region, revenue DESC)","GROUP BY region, rep_id HAVING revenue = MAX(revenue)","DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY revenue DESC)"]'::jsonb, 3),
('data_02', 'Missing Value Imputation & Outlier Detection', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'You are analyzing customer annual income data from a retail survey. The income distribution is heavily right-skewed with extreme positive outliers representing billionaire customers, as well as several missing values.

Which central tendency metric and outlier detection method are most robust and appropriate for this right-skewed distribution?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_analyst', 'Data Wrangling & Statistics', 20, 'Right-skewed continuous income distribution with missing records and positive outliers', 'Robust statistical metrics for central tendency and anomaly bounds', 'In right-skewed data, extreme upper-tail values inflate the arithmetic mean and standard deviation. The Median provides a robust measure of central tendency for imputation, and the Interquartile Range rule (bounds at Q1 - 1.5*IQR and Q3 + 1.5*IQR) identifies outliers without assuming a normal bell curve.', 'Asymmetric, heavily skewed univariate distribution with extreme upper-tail outliers.', 'The arithmetic mean and standard deviation are sensitive to extreme outliers. The median and interquartile range (IQR) are non-parametric and resistant to skew.', '["Impute missing values with the Median and detect outliers using the 1.5 * IQR Interquartile Range method","Impute missing values with the arithmetic Mean and detect outliers using +/- 3 Standard Deviations (Z-score)","Delete all rows containing values above the mean and replace missing values with zero","Impute missing values using the statistical Mode and remove the top 50% of records"]'::jsonb, 0),
('da_01', 'SQL Rolling Averages & Cumulative Metric Tracking', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'An executive dashboard tracks daily online orders from a daily_sales table with columns (sale_date, daily_revenue). To smooth out weekend fluctuations, the leadership team requests a 7-day rolling average of daily revenue calculated for each day (the average of the current day plus the preceding 6 days).

Which SQL window frame specification correctly calculates this 7-day moving average?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_analyst', 'Advanced SQL', 20, 'Schema: daily_sales(sale_date DATE, daily_revenue NUMERIC) ordered sequentially', 'Window frame specification spanning current day and 6 preceding rows', 'The window frame ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW instructs the database engine to include the current row and the 6 prior rows (7 total daily values) when computing AVG(daily_revenue).', 'Continuous daily time series ordered chronologically by sale_date.', 'A 7-day rolling window consists of 7 total rows: the current row plus the 6 preceding rows in chronological order.', '["AVG(daily_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY sale_date ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)","AVG(daily_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY sale_date ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)","AVG(daily_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY sale_date ROWS BETWEEN 7 PRECEDING AND 1 FOLLOWING)","AVG(daily_revenue) GROUP BY DATEADD(day, 7, sale_date)"]'::jsonb, 1),
('da_02', 'Customer Cohort Retention & Churn Analysis', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'A SaaS subscription company began the month of January with exactly 2,000 active paying subscribers. During January, 100 existing subscribers cancelled their accounts, and the marketing team acquired 300 brand new subscribers, ending January with 2,200 active subscribers.

What is the customer churn rate for the month of January, and which formula defines it?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_analyst', 'Business Analytics', 20, 'Start of month subscribers = 2,000; Cancellations = 100; New acquisitions = 300', 'Monthly customer churn rate percentage and metric definition', 'Customer Churn Rate is calculated as (Customers Lost During Period) / (Customers at Start of Period) = 100 / 2,000 = 0.05 = 5.0%. Newly acquired customers are counted in acquisition cohorts and do not alter the starting denominator of the churn formula.', 'Standard subscription cohort metric definitions over a monthly billing period.', 'Customer churn rate evaluates the percentage of customers present at the start of the period who cancelled during that period.', '["15.0%, calculated as (New Customers Acquired) / (Customers at Start of Period) = 300 / 2,000","4.5%, calculated as (Customers Lost During Period) / (Customers at End of Period) = 100 / 2,200","5.0%, calculated as (Customers Lost During Period) / (Customers at Start of Period) = 100 / 2,000","33.3%, calculated as (Customers Lost During Period) / (New Customers Acquired) = 100 / 300"]'::jsonb, 2),
('da_03', 'Exploratory Data Analysis & Visual Distribution Skew', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'You generate a histogram and summary statistics for a company''s customer order value data. You notice that the histogram displays a long tail extending far to the right, and the statistical summary reveals Mean = $145, Median = $62, and Mode = $40.

How is this distribution classified, and what does the relationship between Mean, Median, and Mode signify?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'data_analyst', 'Data Visualization', 20, 'Distribution summary: Mean = 45, Median = 2, Mode = 0 with right tail', 'Statistical classification of skewness direction and measure relationships', 'A distribution with a long right tail is Positively (Right) Skewed. Infrequent, exceptionally high values pull the arithmetic Mean significantly to the right, while the Median remains resistant and the Mode reflects the most frequent lower price points, establishing Mean > Median > Mode.', 'Unimodal continuous financial distribution.', 'When extreme high values pull the arithmetic average upward to the right, the distribution is right-skewed, causing Mean > Median > Mode.', '["Negatively (Left) Skewed distribution where Mode > Median > Mean","Perfectly Symmetrical Normal Distribution where Mean = Median = Mode","Bimodal Uniform Distribution where variance is zero","Positively (Right) Skewed distribution where Mean > Median > Mode"]'::jsonb, 3),
('uiux_01', 'WCAG 2.1 AA Contrast Ratio & Touch Sizing', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'You are designing the design system for a mobile application that must comply with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG 2.1 Level AA) and Google Material Design touch ergonomics.

What is the minimum required visual color contrast ratio for standard body text (< 18pt regular font), and what is the minimum recommended touch target dimension for interactive components?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'uiux_designer', 'Accessibility & Inclusive Design', 20, 'Mobile application UI components evaluated for WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance', 'Standard text contrast threshold and minimum interactive target size in dp', 'WCAG 2.1 Level AA mandates a minimum luminance contrast ratio of 4.5:1 against the background for normal text (under 18pt or 14pt bold). For mobile touch accessibility, Material Design 3 and Android standards require interactive touch targets to be at least 48 x 48 dp to accommodate human fingertip interaction.', 'WCAG 2.1 Level AA standards and Android Material 3 touch target guidelines.', 'WCAG AA requires a 4.5:1 contrast ratio for normal text (3:1 for large text >= 18pt), and mobile guidelines specify at least 48x48 dp for reliable tapping.', '["4.5:1 minimum contrast ratio for standard body text, and 48 x 48 dp minimum touch target size","3.0:1 minimum contrast ratio for body text, and 32 x 32 dp minimum touch target size","7.0:1 minimum contrast ratio for body text, and 24 x 24 dp minimum touch target size","2.0:1 minimum contrast ratio for body text, and 64 x 64 dp minimum touch target size"]'::jsonb, 0),
('uiux_02', 'Information Architecture & Card Sorting Analysis', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A design team is reorganizing the navigation structure of an enterprise healthcare portal with over 60 distinct features. The team needs to understand how real users naturally group these healthcare services into mental categories before establishing primary menu categories.

Which research methodology should the team execute, and how do Open versus Closed card sorting differ?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'uiux_designer', 'Information Architecture', 20, 'Navigation architecture evaluation: comparison of open vs. closed card sorting', 'Methodological differences and appropriate generative research applications', 'Card sorting is the standard UX research method for discovering mental models. In an Open Card Sort, participants group feature cards and create their own category names, uncovering user terminology. In a Closed Card Sort, participants place cards into predefined category buckets to validate an established structure.', 'Qualitative and quantitative UX information architecture research standards.', 'Open card sorting is generative (participants create their own category labels), whereas closed card sorting is evaluative (participants sort cards into existing buckets).', '["Closed card sorting lets participants create new categories, while open card sorting uses fixed labels","Open card sorting allows participants to create category labels; closed card sorting sorts into predefined categories","Card sorting is exclusively used to select UI color schemes and font hierarchies","Tree testing is used to create categories, while open card sorting is used to test server latency"]'::jsonb, 1),
('uiux_03', 'Usability Testing & System Usability Scale (SUS)', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'Following a comprehensive usability test of a redesigned mobile banking app, 25 participants completed the standardized 10-item System Usability Scale (SUS) questionnaire with 5-point Likert scales.

What is the industry-standard benchmark SUS score that represents average (50th percentile) usability across software products?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'uiux_designer', 'UX Research', 20, 'Standard 10-item SUS questionnaire administered across 30 usability test participants', 'Recognized industry benchmark score corresponding to average 50th percentile usability', 'Based on empirical research across over 500 usability studies (Brooke, Bangor et al.), the average System Usability Scale (SUS) score is 68. Scores above 68 are considered above average (e.g., 80.3+ is an ''A'' grade), while scores below 68 indicate usability deficiencies requiring remediation.', 'Standard Brooke/Sauro System Usability Scale methodology on 0-100 scale.', 'An SUS score of 68 is widely established in UX literature as the average score (equivalent to a ''C'' grade).', '["50.0 is the standardized average baseline score on the 0-100 SUS scale","85.0 is the minimum threshold required for a system to be considered usable","68.0 is the recognized industry average benchmark score representing 50th percentile usability","35.0 is the typical average score achieved by consumer mobile applications"]'::jsonb, 2),
('uiux_04', 'Design Systems & Responsive Modular Grid Scaling', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'When building a scalable UI design system for cross-platform Android and web applications, design systems engineers standardize spacing, padding, margins, and component dimensions using a base 8dp/8px spatial grid.

Why is the 8dp baseline grid the universal standard in modern UI design systems rather than arbitrary pixel values (such as 7px or 13px)?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'uiux_designer', 'Design Systems', 20, 'Design system spatial token architecture using base grid units', 'Mathematical and display rendering benefits of 8dp multiples', 'Modern displays use varied pixel densities (@1x, @1.5x, @2x, @3x, @4x). Because 8 is an even number divisible by 2 and 4, dimensions using multiples of 8dp scale into clean integer physical pixel counts without creating fractional sub-pixels, preventing visual blur and ensuring cross-platform rendering consistency.', 'Cross-platform rendering across varied device pixel densities.', 'Multiples of 8 divide and scale evenly into whole integers across common device density multipliers (@1x, @2x, @3x, @4x), preventing blurry sub-pixel rendering.', '["Arbitrary odd pixel values like 7px provide superior visual asymmetry on mobile screens","The 8dp grid is mandatory because mobile CPU hardware only computes bytes of 8 bits","CSS and Android layouts do not permit margin values that are not powers of two","Multiples of 8dp scale cleanly into whole integer pixels across common device density factors (@1x to @4x)"]'::jsonb, 3),
('uiux_05', 'Cognitive Load Reduction & Interaction Micro-States', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'An e-commerce checkout page presents users with 25 distinct form fields and decision options on a single overwhelming screen, resulting in high shopping cart abandonment. The design team refactors the checkout into a clear, 3-step progressive wizard with chunked inputs.

Which fundamental UX psychological law directly explains why reducing the number of immediate choices decreases user decision time and reduces cognitive friction?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'uiux_designer', 'Interaction Design', 20, 'User decision time evaluation under varying counts of interface choices', 'Foundational HCI psychological principle governing decision latency', 'Hick''s Law states that the time it takes for a user to make a decision increases logarithmically with the number and complexity of choices: T = b * log_2(n + 1). Breaking 25 choices into chunked progressive steps lowers the cognitive load n at each step, significantly accelerating decision velocity and conversion.', 'Standard human-computer interaction (HCI) cognitive principles.', 'Hick''s Law (Hick-Hyman Law) describes the time it takes to make a decision as a function of the number of possible choices.', '["Hick''s Law, which states that decision time increases logarithmically with the number of options presented","Fitts''s Law, which states that larger buttons always produce faster checkout decisions","Jakob''s Law, which dictates that every checkout flow must look identical to Amazon","Miller''s Law, which states that users can effortlessly retain 50 items in working memory"]'::jsonb, 0),
('cyber_01', 'OWASP Top 10: SQL Injection & XSS Defense', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'During a security audit of a web application, you find the following backend code: query = "SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE user_id = ''" + request.getParameter("id") + "''". An attacker inputs id = '' OR ''1''=''1'' -- to bypass authentication and dump the entire database.

Which primary defense mechanism completely neutralizes SQL Injection vulnerabilities at the architecture level?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cyber_analyst', 'Application Security', 20, 'Untrusted user input passed into database authentication queries', 'Definitive secure coding defense against SQL injection attacks', 'Parameterized queries (Prepared Statements with bind variables) ensure the database engine compiles the SQL statement structure beforehand and treats all user inputs strictly as literal data rather than executable SQL syntax, completely preventing SQL injection regardless of special characters.', 'OWASP Top 10 Application Security Standards (A03:2021-Injection).', 'Parameterized queries separate the SQL executable code structure from user-provided data parameters.', '["Filtering single quote characters with a simple client-side JavaScript regex replace function","Using Parameterized Prepared Statements with bound parameters so user input is never executed as SQL code","Storing all database credentials in plain text configuration files","Encoding backend SQL error messages into base64 before logging them"]'::jsonb, 1),
('cyber_02', 'Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) & TLS Handshake', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'When a web browser establishes a secure HTTPS session with a bank web server using TLS 1.3, asymmetric cryptography and symmetric cryptography are both utilized.

Why does TLS use asymmetric cryptography during the initial handshake, but switch to symmetric encryption (such as AES-GCM) for the actual session data transmission?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cyber_analyst', 'Cryptography & Network Security', 20, 'Secure HTTPS connection initialization and session payload transmission', 'Roles of asymmetric and symmetric cryptographic schemes during connection lifecycle', 'Asymmetric cryptography (certificates and public/private key pairs) authenticates server identity and securely negotiates shared secrets without prior trust. Once negotiated, symmetric encryption (like AES-256-GCM) is used for bulk data transfer because it is several orders of magnitude faster and hardware-accelerated.', 'TLS 1.2 / TLS 1.3 protocol standards and cryptographic performance trade-offs.', 'Asymmetric encryption (public/private keys) is computationally expensive; symmetric encryption is orders of magnitude faster for high-volume streaming data.', '["Symmetric encryption is used for server authentication, while asymmetric encryption encrypts data payloads","Asymmetric encryption is faster than symmetric encryption for large streaming video files","Asymmetric crypto authenticates identity and exchanges keys; symmetric crypto efficiently encrypts session data payloads","Symmetric keys must be transmitted in plain text across the network during every request"]'::jsonb, 2),
('cyber_03', 'Network Traffic Forensics & Packet Inspection', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A Security Operations Center (SOC) analyst inspects network firewall logs and observes an external IP address sending 200,000 TCP SYN packets per second to port 443 with spoofed source IPs, but never returning any corresponding ACK packets to complete the TCP three-way handshake.

What type of cyber attack is occurring, and what server resource does it exhaust?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cyber_analyst', 'Network Forensics', 20, 'Network traffic: Influx of TCP SYN packets with spoofed IPs and no final ACKs', 'Attack taxonomy identification and targeted operating system resource exhaustion', 'This is a classic TCP SYN Flood Denial of Service (DoS) attack. The attacker exploits the TCP 3-way handshake by sending rapid SYN packets without sending the final ACK, filling the target server''s half-open connection backlog table and preventing legitimate clients from connecting.', 'TCP/IP state machine dynamics (RFC 793 / RFC 4987).', 'A SYN flood leaves half-open connections waiting in the server''s connection backlog table until memory resources are exhausted.', '["Normal HTTP/2 bidirectional video streaming traffic","Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) ARP cache poisoning attack on the local switch","SQL Injection payload delivery over DNS port 53","TCP SYN Flood Denial of Service (DoS) attack exhausting the server''s half-open connection backlog queue"]'::jsonb, 3),
('cyber_04', 'Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) & OAuth 2.0 Flow', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'A mobile banking app needs to allow users to authorize access to their financial transactions on a third-party budgeting service. Because mobile applications are public clients that cannot securely store a private client_secret, standard Authorization Code Flow is vulnerable to code interception.

Which OAuth 2.0 extension protocol is mandatory for securing public mobile and Single-Page applications?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cyber_analyst', 'Identity & Access Management', 20, 'Public mobile app requesting delegated API access without hardcoded client secrets', 'Standard RFC 7636 authorization grant flow for public native clients', 'Authorization Code Flow with Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE, RFC 7636) prevents authorization code interception on public clients. The client creates a dynamic cryptographic code_verifier and code_challenge, allowing the authorization server to verify the token requester without requiring a static client secret.', 'OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (RFC 7636 / RFC 8252).', 'PKCE dynamically creates a cryptographic code_verifier and code_challenge for each authorization request.', '["Authorization Code Flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) using dynamic cryptographic challenges","OAuth 2.0 Implicit Grant flow returning access tokens directly in URL hash fragments","Resource Owner Password Credentials Grant sharing the user''s raw password directly with third parties","Client Credentials Grant using a hardcoded secret in the mobile application APK"]'::jsonb, 0),
('cyber_05', 'Incident Response Playbook & SIEM IoC Correlation', 'Technical', 'Hard', 'A Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system detects confirmed ransomware executing on three enterprise workstations and attempting lateral movement across the internal subnet. The SOC incident response team must immediately follow the standard NIST SP 800-61 incident handling lifecycle.

What is the correct sequential order of the NIST Incident Response lifecycle phases?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cyber_analyst', 'Security Operations', 20, 'Enterprise security breach requiring structured incident response execution', 'Chronological lifecycle phases according to NIST SP 800-61 framework', 'NIST SP 800-61 defines the 4 core phases: 1) Preparation, 2) Detection & Analysis, 3) Containment, Eradication & Recovery, and 4) Post-Incident Activity (Lessons Learned). When active malware is detected, immediate Containment prevents lateral spread before Eradication and system Recovery.', 'NIST Special Publication 800-61 Rev. 2 Incident Handling Lifecycle.', 'You must detect the threat, contain it to prevent spreading, eradicate it, recover systems, and conduct post-incident lessons learned.', '["Eradication -> Preparation -> Post-Incident Review -> Detection & Analysis","Preparation -> Detection & Analysis -> Containment, Eradication & Recovery -> Post-Incident Activity","Recovery -> Eradication -> Containment -> Detection -> Preparation","Detection -> System Reformat -> Public Press Release -> Network Rebuild"]'::jsonb, 1),
('cloud_01', 'Docker Containerization & Multi-Stage Builds', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'You are creating a Dockerfile for a production Java Spring Boot microservice. Compiling the application requires Maven (500 MB) and a full JDK (400 MB), but running the compiled JAR file only requires a minimal Java Runtime Environment (JRE Alpine, 80 MB).

Which Dockerfile design pattern allows you to build the application with full build tools while keeping the final production container image minimal and secure?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cloud_engineer', 'Containers & CI/CD', 20, 'Application build pipeline: Maven compiler + JDK -> Final runnable JAR on Alpine JRE', 'Docker build pattern optimizing image size and security posture', 'Docker Multi-Stage Builds use multiple FROM statements (e.g. ''FROM maven AS builder'' and ''FROM eclipse-temurin:alpine AS runner''). The final stage copies only the compiled JAR artifact via ''COPY --from=builder'', leaving compilers, debuggers, and source code out of the production container, drastically reducing image size and attack surface.', 'Production OCI container image packaging standards.', 'Multi-stage builds use multiple FROM instructions in a single Dockerfile, copying only artifacts from the build stage to the final runtime image.', '["Installing all compilers, debuggers, and source code directly into the production container","Running container processes strictly as the root user with host filesystem mounts","Multi-Stage Builds that separate the build environment from the final lean runtime container image","Storing database credentials directly in environment variables committed to public Git repositories"]'::jsonb, 2),
('cloud_02', 'Kubernetes Pod Auto-Scaling & Load Balancing', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A microservice deployed on a Kubernetes production cluster experiences sudden traffic surges during flash sales, causing pod CPU utilization to spike from 30% to 90%. The infrastructure team needs the deployment to automatically scale out the number of running pod replicas when average CPU exceeds 70%.

Which Kubernetes native resource is designed to monitor metrics and automatically adjust the replica count of a Deployment?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cloud_engineer', 'Cloud Orchestration', 20, 'Deployment with variable incoming traffic and target CPU utilization threshold', 'Native Kubernetes controller responsible for dynamic horizontal pod scaling', 'The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) continuously monitors resource metrics (such as CPU or Memory utilization via the Metrics Server) and automatically adjusts the replicas field of a Deployment or ReplicaSet to match the target utilization threshold (e.g., 70% CPU).', 'Kubernetes core autoscaling API (autoscaling/v2) with metrics-server enabled.', 'HPA scales out (adds pod replicas) based on observed metric utilization such as CPU or memory.', '["Cluster Autoscaler, which immediately restarts worker nodes when pods experience high traffic","Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA), which reduces memory limits during peak traffic periods","Kubernetes Ingress Controller, which rejects all incoming HTTP requests above 100 req/s","Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA), which dynamically scales the number of pod replicas based on CPU/memory metrics"]'::jsonb, 3),
('cloud_03', 'Linux Administration & Graceful Process Signals', 'Technical', 'Easy', 'When deploying a new version of a web application running as a Linux systemd service or Docker container, the orchestration orchestrator must instruct the active application process to stop accepting new requests, finish processing in-flight database transactions, and terminate cleanly.

Which POSIX signal should be sent first to initiate a graceful application shutdown, and which signal forcefully kills the process if it fails to exit?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cloud_engineer', 'Linux Fundamentals', 20, 'Process termination request with graceful cleanup timeout period', 'POSIX signal pair for graceful notification and forceful termination', 'SIGTERM (Signal 15) is the standard termination signal that notifies the application to execute cleanup handlers, close network connections, and exit gracefully. If the process does not terminate within a timeout period, SIGKILL (Signal 9) is issued, which is handled directly by the OS kernel and terminates the process immediately.', 'Standard POSIX operating system process signals.', 'SIGTERM (15) can be caught and handled by application cleanup routines. SIGKILL (9) cannot be caught or ignored.', '["Send SIGTERM (Signal 15) for graceful shutdown, followed by SIGKILL (Signal 9) if the process hangs","Send SIGKILL (Signal 9) for graceful cleanup, followed by SIGTERM (Signal 15) for forced termination","Send SIGHUP (Signal 1) to immediately shut down the physical Linux server","Send SIGSTOP (Signal 19) to permanently delete the application''s binary from the disk"]'::jsonb, 0),
('cloud_04', 'VPC Subnetting & High-Availability Network Design', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'You are designing the network architecture for a 3-tier enterprise cloud application in AWS or GCP. The production database cluster contains sensitive financial customer records. The database servers must be able to initiate outbound connections to the Internet to download operating system security updates, but they must NEVER be accessible from the public Internet.

Which Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) subnet and routing configuration fulfills these security requirements?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cloud_engineer', 'Cloud Networking', 20, 'Tier-3 database cluster requiring outbound update access with zero inbound internet ingress', 'Secure VPC subnetting and routing architecture satisfying isolation requirements', 'The database instances are placed in a Private Subnet without public IPv4 addresses. The private subnet''s route table directs default outbound traffic (0.0.0.0/0) to a Network Address Translation (NAT) Gateway residing in a Public Subnet. The NAT Gateway forwards outbound update requests to the Internet without allowing any unsolicited inbound connections.', 'Cloud VPC architecture adhering to the Principle of Least Privilege.', 'Private subnets lack public IP addresses and direct Internet Gateway routes. Outbound traffic is routed through a Network Address Translation (NAT) Gateway in a public subnet.', '["Assign public IP addresses to database nodes and attach an Internet Gateway directly to the private subnet","Place database servers in a Private Subnet and route outbound traffic through a NAT Gateway in a Public Subnet","Place database instances in a Public Subnet with open security group ingress on port 5432","Disable all routing tables and DNS resolution across the entire VPC"]'::jsonb, 1),
('cloud_05', 'CI/CD Pipeline Automation & Blue-Green Traffic Shifting', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'An enterprise SaaS platform cannot tolerate any downtime during production releases. The engineering team implements a Blue-Green Deployment strategy to deploy version 2.0 while version 1.0 is currently handling live production traffic.

How does a Blue-Green deployment achieve zero-downtime releases and rapid rollback capability?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', 'cloud_engineer', 'DevOps & CI/CD', 20, 'Live production traffic on active environment with new release staged in parallel', 'Operational mechanism of Blue-Green deployment and instant rollback', 'In Blue-Green deployment, two identical production environments exist. Version 1.0 runs in Blue while Version 2.0 is deployed and verified in Green. Once Green passes automated health checks, the load balancer/router switches traffic from Blue to Green instantaneously with zero downtime. If issues arise, traffic is instantly reverted back to Blue.', 'Zero-downtime deployment strategy with immediate rollback capability.', 'Two identical production environments exist. Traffic is switched instantaneously at the load balancer level once the new environment is validated.', '["Taking down the entire production cluster for a scheduled 2-hour maintenance outage","Deploying untested code changes directly to the production database during peak business hours","Deploying to an identical Green environment and switching load balancer traffic instantaneously from Blue to Green","Gradually overwriting server binaries in place without checking application health endpoints"]'::jsonb, 2),
('apt_01', 'Time and Work: Alternate Work Days', 'Aptitude', 'Medium', 'A can complete a piece of work in 10 days, while B can complete the same work in 15 days. They work on alternate days, with A working on Day 1 and B working on Day 2. They continue this alternating pattern (A on odd days, B on even days) until the entire work is completed.

How many total days will they take to complete the entire work?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Time and Work', 20, 'Individual capacities: A = 10 days, B = 15 days. Alternating sequence: Day 1 (A), Day 2 (B)...', 'Total number of days required to complete all work units', 'Let total work = LCM(10, 15) = 30 units.
A''s 1-day work = 30 / 10 = 3 units/day.
B''s 1-day work = 30 / 15 = 2 units/day.
In one 2-day cycle (Day 1 by A + Day 2 by B), work completed = 3 + 2 = 5 units.
Number of 2-day cycles required = 30 / 5 = 6 complete cycles.
Total days taken = 6 cycles × 2 days/cycle = 12 days.', 'Both workers maintain uniform, constant productivity rates throughout.', 'Find the total work using the LCM of 10 and 15 (30 units). Then determine the work completed in one 2-day cycle (A''s daily work + B''s daily work).', '["10 days","14 days","15 days","12 days"]'::jsonb, 3),
('apt_02', 'Probability: Two Cards of the Same Suit', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'Two cards are drawn randomly one after another without replacement from a standard, well-shuffled pack of 52 playing cards (containing 4 suits with 13 cards in each suit).

What is the exact probability that both drawn cards belong to the same suit?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Probability', 20, 'Deck: 52 cards, 4 suits of 13 cards each. Sequential draw of 2 cards without replacement.', 'Probability of both cards sharing identical suit', 'The first card drawn can be any card from any suit (probability = 52/52 = 1). Once the first card is drawn, exactly 12 cards of that same suit remain in the deck out of 51 total remaining cards. Therefore, Probability = 1 × (12 / 51) = 4 / 17 ≈ 23.53%.', 'Standard deck composition without jokers. Sampling without replacement.', 'The first card drawn can be of any suit (probability = 52/52 = 1). For the second card to match that same suit, calculate how many cards of that suit remain out of the remaining total cards.', '["4/17 (~23.5%)","1/4 (25.0%)","1/13 (~7.7%)","1/17 (~5.9%)"]'::jsonb, 0),
('apt_03', 'Problems on Trains: Crossing in Opposite Directions', 'Aptitude', 'Medium', 'Two trains with lengths of 150 meters and 200 meters are running on parallel railway tracks in opposite directions toward each other. Train 1 travels at a uniform speed of 54 km/h and Train 2 travels at a uniform speed of 72 km/h.

How many seconds will they take to completely cross each other from the moment their front engines meet?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Time, Speed and Distance', 20, 'Train lengths: 150 m and 200 m. Speeds: 54 km/h and 72 km/h moving in opposite directions.', 'Time in seconds for the two trains to completely pass each other', 'Total distance to completely cross each other = Length1 + Length2 = 150 m + 200 m = 350 meters.
Relative Speed in opposite directions = Speed1 + Speed2 = 54 + 72 = 126 km/h.
Converting to meters per second: 126 × (5 / 18) = 7 × 5 = 35 m/s.
Time taken = Total Distance / Relative Speed = 350 / 35 = 10 seconds.', 'Constant speeds on parallel tracks. Unit conversion: 1 km/h = 5/18 m/s.', 'When moving in opposite directions, the relative speed is the sum of both speeds. The total distance to cross is the sum of both train lengths.', '["8 seconds","10 seconds","12 seconds","14 seconds"]'::jsonb, 1),
('apt_04', 'Profit and Loss: Successive Discounts', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'A store manager marks a designer jacket at a Marked Price (MP) of $500. During a festive sale, the store offers two successive discounts of 20% and 10% on the marked price.

What is the single equivalent discount percentage, and what is the final selling price paid by a customer?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Profit and Loss', 20, 'Marked Price = 00 with consecutive discounts of 20% followed by 10%', 'Equivalent single discount percentage and final discounted purchase price', 'Single Equivalent Discount D = d1 + d2 - (d1 × d2) / 100 = 20 + 10 - (20 × 10) / 100 = 30 - 2 = 28%.
Final Selling Price = Marked Price × (1 - D/100) = $500 × (1 - 0.28) = $500 × 0.72 = $360.
(Alternatively: After 20% discount on $500 = $400; after 10% discount on $400 = $400 - $40 = $360).', 'Successive discounts applied sequentially to the reducing balance.', 'Use the successive discount formula: Equivalent Discount D = d1 + d2 - (d1 × d2) / 100.', '["30% discount, Final Selling Price = $350","25% discount, Final Selling Price = $375","28% discount, Final Selling Price = $360","22% discount, Final Selling Price = $390"]'::jsonb, 2),
('apt_05', 'Problems on Ages: Father and Son', 'Aptitude', 'Medium', 'The ratio of the present ages of a father and his son is 5 : 2. Exactly 5 years from now, the sum of their ages will be 80 years.

What is the present age of the father?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Ages', 20, 'Current age ratio Father : Son = 5 : 2. Combined age in 5 years = 80 years.', 'Present age of the father in years', 'Let Father''s present age = 5x and Son''s present age = 2x.
In 5 years, Father''s age = 5x + 5, and Son''s age = 2x + 5.
Given sum in 5 years: (5x + 5) + (2x + 5) = 80
=> 7x + 10 = 80
=> 7x = 70
=> x = 10.
Therefore, Father''s present age = 5x = 5 × 10 = 50 years (and Son''s present age is 20 years).', 'Both individuals age at the identical rate of 1 year per calendar year.', 'Let the present ages of father and son be 5x and 2x. In 5 years, their ages will be (5x + 5) and (2x + 5). Set their sum equal to 80.', '["45 years","55 years","60 years","50 years"]'::jsonb, 3),
('apt_06', 'Pipes and Cisterns: Two Inflow Pipes', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'Pipe A can fill an empty water reservoir in 6 hours, while Pipe B can fill the same empty reservoir in 8 hours. If both pipes are opened simultaneously into the empty reservoir, how many hours will they take to fill the reservoir completely?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Pipes and Cisterns', 20, 'Filling times: Pipe A = 6 hours, Pipe B = 8 hours running simultaneously', 'Total elapsed time in hours to fill the cistern completely', 'Work done by Pipe A in 1 hour = 1/6 of reservoir.
Work done by Pipe B in 1 hour = 1/8 of reservoir.
Combined work done by (A + B) in 1 hour = 1/6 + 1/8 = (4 + 3) / 24 = 7/24 of reservoir.
Total time required to fill reservoir completely = 1 / (7/24) = 24/7 hours = 3 3/7 hours (approx. 3 hours 25.7 minutes).', 'Uniform flow rates with zero leakage or evaporation.', 'Calculate the 1-hour filling rate of each pipe (1/6 and 1/8), add them to find the combined 1-hour rate, and take the reciprocal.', '["3 3/7 hours (24/7 hours)","3 hours","4 hours","7 hours"]'::jsonb, 0),
('apt_07', 'Ratio and Proportion: Mixture Dilution', 'Aptitude', 'Medium', 'A container holds 60 liters of a milk and water mixture where the ratio of milk to water is 2 : 1. A dairy worker wants to dilute the mixture by adding pure water so that the new ratio of milk to water becomes 1 : 2.

How many liters of pure water must be added to the mixture?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Ratio and Proportion', 20, 'Initial mixture = 60 L with Milk : Water = 2 : 1. Target ratio Milk : Water = 1 : 2.', 'Quantity of pure water in liters added to achieve target ratio', 'Initial volume = 60 L with Milk : Water = 2 : 1.
Initial Milk = (2/3) × 60 = 40 liters.
Initial Water = (1/3) × 60 = 20 liters.
Let x liters of water be added to achieve Milk : Water = 1 : 2:
40 / (20 + x) = 1 / 2
=> 20 + x = 80
=> x = 60 liters of water.', 'Milk volume remains strictly constant throughout the dilution process.', 'Calculate the initial quantities of milk (40 L) and water (20 L). Set up the equation: Milk / (Water + x) = 1 / 2.', '["40 liters","60 liters","80 liters","50 liters"]'::jsonb, 1),
('apt_08', 'Simple Interest: Multiplication of Principal', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'A sum of money invested under Simple Interest at a constant annual interest rate doubles itself in exactly 8 years.

In how many total years will the principal amount become 4 times its original value at the exact same rate of simple interest?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Simple and Compound Interest', 20, 'Principal doubles (Amount = 2P) in 8 years under constant simple interest', 'Total investment tenure in years to quadruple initial principal', 'Let the principal be P. When it doubles in 8 years, the Amount A = 2P, so Simple Interest earned SI_1 = 2P - P = P in 8 years.
To become 4 times the original principal, Amount A = 4P, so required Simple Interest SI_2 = 4P - P = 3P.
Because Simple Interest is directly proportional to time (T), earning 3 times the interest takes 3 × 8 years = 24 years.', 'Constant annual interest rate with non-compounding simple interest.', 'When principal doubles, Simple Interest earned = P in 8 years. To become 4 times the principal (Amount = 4P), interest earned must be 3P.', '["16 years","32 years","24 years","20 years"]'::jsonb, 2),
('apt_09', 'Averages: Team Batting Average Change', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'In a cricket match, the average score of the first 5 batsmen was 50 runs. When the 6th batsman batted and his score was included, the overall average score of all 6 batsmen increased to 55 runs.

How many runs did the 6th batsman score in the match?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Average', 20, '5 batsmen average 50 runs. With 6th batsman, 6-player average becomes 55 runs.', 'Individual score of 6th batsman required to produce average increase', 'Total runs scored by 5 batsmen = 5 × 50 = 250 runs.
Total runs scored by 6 batsmen = 6 × 55 = 330 runs.
Runs scored by the 6th batsman = Total(6) - Total(5) = 330 - 250 = 80 runs.', 'All scores are non-negative integers.', 'Calculate the total runs scored by 5 batsmen (5 × 50) and the total runs scored by 6 batsmen (6 × 55), then find the difference.', '["75 runs","85 runs","90 runs","80 runs"]'::jsonb, 3),
('apt_10', 'Percentages: Price Increase & Consumption Adjustment', 'Aptitude', 'Easy', 'Due to global market inflation, the retail price of petrol increases by 25%. A vehicle owner wants to adjust their monthly driving habits so that their total financial expenditure on petrol remains exactly unchanged.

By what percentage must the owner reduce their petrol consumption to keep expenditure constant?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Percentages', 20, 'Price increase = 25%. Budget expenditure is held strictly constant.', 'Percentage reduction in consumption required to maintain constant spend', 'Let initial Price = $100 and Consumption = 100 units (Expenditure = $10,000).
New Price = $125. To keep expenditure at $10,000, new Consumption = 10,000 / 125 = 80 units.
Reduction in consumption = 100 - 80 = 20 units = 20%.
Formula: [r / (100 + r)] × 100% = [25 / (100 + 25)] × 100% = (25 / 125) × 100% = (1/5) × 100% = 20%.', 'Expenditure = Price * Consumption = Constant.', 'Use the standard expenditure conservation formula: Percentage Reduction = [r / (100 + r)] × 100%, where r is the price increase percentage.', '["20% reduction","25% reduction","16.67% reduction","15% reduction"]'::jsonb, 0),
('iv_01', 'STAR Method: Resolving Technical Disagreements', 'Interview', 'Easy', 'During a behavioral interview for a software engineering position, the interviewer asks: ''Tell me about a time you had a strong technical disagreement with a teammate on an architectural decision. How did you handle it?''

Which structured communication strategy and behavioral approach demonstrates the highest level of professional maturity and engineering leadership?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Behavioral Interviews', 20, 'Behavioral interview prompt regarding engineering disagreements and conflict resolution', 'Structured behavioral response framework and constructive resolution strategy', 'The STAR framework (Situation, Task, Action, Result) provides a clear narrative. A strong answer highlights active listening, depersonalizing the conflict by building prototypes or benchmarking metrics, aligning on customer requirements, and achieving a successful measurable outcome while maintaining team cohesion.', 'Standard software engineering behavioral competency interview evaluation.', 'Structure your response using the STAR method: Situation, Task, collaborative data-driven Action, and positive business Result.', '["Explain why your architecture was objectively superior and how you convinced management to overrule your peer","Use the STAR method: explain the Situation, Task, collaborative data-driven Action (prototyping/benchmarks), and positive Result","State that you always immediately concede and adopt the other person''s proposal to avoid workplace conflict","Focus primarily on describing the teammate''s lack of technical competence to justify your position"]'::jsonb, 1),
('iv_02', 'System Design: Mitigating Read-Heavy Database Bottlenecks', 'Interview', 'Medium', 'In a system design interview, you are asked to scale an e-commerce platform. During flash sale events, the platform experiences severe database read latency spikes where 92% of incoming queries are repeated reads of product catalog data, pushing the primary relational database CPU to 95%.

Which architectural strategy is most appropriate and cost-effective as the initial mitigation to relieve read database pressure?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'System Design', 20, 'Workload profile: 92% read traffic, 8% write traffic with saturated primary database CPU', 'Optimal architectural mitigation for read-heavy database bottlenecks', 'For read-heavy workloads (90%+ reads), introducing an in-memory caching layer (e.g. Redis or Memcached) with a cache-aside pattern intercepts the vast majority of repetitive catalog queries in sub-millisecond time. Adding horizontal Read Replicas distributes remaining uncached read traffic, freeing the primary database exclusively for critical transactional writes.', 'High read-to-write ratio (92:8). Minimal migration risk and high availability required.', 'Offload read traffic from the primary database by caching popular product records in an in-memory cache and routing remaining read queries to read replicas.', '["Immediately decompose the entire application into 25 microservices with separate NoSQL databases","Replace the relational database with unindexed flat CSV files stored on local server disks","Implement an in-memory caching layer (Redis) and database Read Replicas to offload repetitive read queries","Disable HTTPS encryption and user authentication headers to reduce database CPU overhead"]'::jsonb, 2),
('iv_03', 'Engineering Leadership: Communicating Technical Debt', 'Interview', 'Easy', 'During a sprint planning meeting, your engineering team identifies that legacy database query code is causing intermittent checkout timeouts and slowing down feature development. Non-technical product managers want to prioritize new customer-facing features instead of refactoring.

How should an engineer effectively communicate and justify allocating sprint capacity toward technical debt reduction to business stakeholders?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Stakeholder Management', 20, 'Legacy codebase causing checkout errors; friction between new features vs. refactoring', 'Effective communication approach bridging engineering realities and business KPIs', 'Product managers prioritize business outcomes. Rather than arguing about code aesthetics, engineers should frame technical debt in terms of business impact: quantified slowdown in future feature delivery velocity, increased probability of revenue-impacting checkout outages, and customer churn.', 'Cross-functional communication between engineering and business/product management.', 'Translate engineering problems into business risks and KPIs: development velocity, customer churn, and revenue loss.', '["Use low-level compiler jargon and abstract design patterns to convince product managers they cannot understand the issue","Quietly refactor the legacy modules in secret on production branches without sprint visibility","Refuse to work on any new product features until the entire codebase is rewritten from scratch","Translate technical debt into business outcomes: future feature delivery velocity, outage risk, and revenue loss"]'::jsonb, 3),
('iv_04', 'Site Reliability: Blameless Post-Mortem Principles', 'Interview', 'Medium', 'A junior engineer accidentally deploys an incorrect environment configuration change that brings down the production billing service for 45 minutes before being rolled back. In a high-performing Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) culture, the team conducts a blameless post-mortem.

What is the primary objective of conducting a blameless post-mortem following a production incident?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Production Operations', 20, 'Production outage caused by accidental misconfiguration during manual deployment', 'Core objective of blameless post-mortem and systemic resilience culture', 'Blameless post-mortems recognize that human error is a symptom of systemic weaknesses in tools, testing, and processes. The primary goal is to identify systemic root causes (e.g., missing pre-flight validation, absence of canary rollouts, or insufficient automated rollback) and build automated guardrails so that a single human mistake cannot cause an outage.', 'SRE principles and incident retrospective culture (Google SRE standard).', 'A blameless post-mortem assumes people act in good faith and focuses on systemic safeguards, automated tests, and deployment guardrails rather than personal blame.', '["Identify systemic root causes, missing automated validation tests, and deployment guardrails to prevent recurrence","Publicly identify and penalize the engineer who triggered the incident to deter future mistakes","Delete all deployment audit logs and server traces to protect the team from executive scrutiny","Restrict all production deployment permissions exclusively to the Chief Executive Officer"]'::jsonb, 0),
('tech_01', 'Database Indexing: B+ Trees vs Hash Indexing', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'You are optimizing a relational database table orders containing 10 million rows. Applications frequently execute range queries such as: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_date BETWEEN ''2026-01-01'' AND ''2026-03-31''.

Why is a B+ Tree index structure significantly superior to a Hash Index for executing this range scan query?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'DBMS', 20, 'Range scan query on 10M rows: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_date BETWEEN ...', 'Algorithmic indexing characteristics enabling efficient range traversals', 'B+ Trees store index entries in sorted order and connect leaf nodes with bidirectional linked pointers. For range queries (BETWEEN, >, <), the B+ Tree performs an O(log N) tree traversal to find the range start and then traverses leaf pages sequentially. Hash indexes use hash functions that scatter values randomly into buckets, only supporting exact equality (=) lookups.', '10 million rows in relational database storage engine (InnoDB/PostgreSQL).', 'B+ Trees maintain keys in sorted order and link leaf pages sequentially, supporting range scans. Hash indexes scatter keys randomly into buckets without ordering.', '["Hash indexes maintain keys in alphabetical sorted order, making them faster for range queries","B+ Trees keep keys sorted and link leaf nodes sequentially, enabling efficient range traversal in O(log N + K) time","B+ Trees have an O(1) worst-case lookup time for every query type","Hash indexes require fewer disk I/O operations than B+ Trees for range scanning queries"]'::jsonb, 1),
('tech_02', 'REST vs gRPC & Protocol Buffers', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'An engineering team is designing high-throughput, low-latency inter-service Remote Procedure Call (RPC) communication between hundreds of backend microservices. They must choose between traditional REST over HTTP/1.1 with JSON payloads and gRPC over HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers.

Which architectural advantages make gRPC with Protocol Buffers significantly faster and more network-efficient than REST with JSON for internal microservices?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Distributed Systems', 20, 'Internal microservice architecture handling 100,000 inter-process RPC calls per second', 'Protobuf serialization and HTTP/2 transport advantages over JSON and HTTP/1.1', 'Protocol Buffers serialize structured data into compact binary payloads that are much smaller and drastically faster to serialize/deserialize than text-based JSON. Furthermore, gRPC uses HTTP/2, which provides bidirectional streaming, header compression (HPACK), and multiplexing of concurrent requests over a single TCP connection.', 'High-throughput, low-latency microservice architecture.', 'Protocol Buffers serialize data into compact binary representations rather than human-readable JSON text, and HTTP/2 supports multiplexing multiple requests over a single TCP connection.', '["JSON text parsing is inherently faster and more compact than binary deserialization","REST over HTTP/1.1 provides built-in bidirectional streaming and header compression by default","gRPC uses compact binary Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 multiplexing, reducing payload size and connection overhead","Protocol Buffers transmit data without any predefined schemas or type contracts"]'::jsonb, 2),
('tech_03', 'Process Synchronization & Mutex vs Semaphore', 'Technical', 'Medium', 'A multi-threaded server application manages access to a fixed pool of N = 5 database connections shared among 50 worker threads. Threads must acquire a connection before executing queries and release it back to the pool immediately upon completion.

What is the key functional difference between a Mutex (Mutual Exclusion lock) and a Counting Semaphore in managing concurrency and resource access?

Select the correct answer from the four options below.', NULL, 'Operating Systems', 20, 'Multi-threaded resource pool: 50 threads competing for N = 5 database connections', 'Functional distinction between ownership locking and resource counting semantics', 'A Mutex is a locking mechanism with thread ownership: only the single thread that locked the mutex can unlock it (strictly 1 thread in critical section). A Counting Semaphore is a signaling mechanism initialized with value N, allowing up to N concurrent threads to acquire permits simultaneously, making it ideal for resource pools.', 'Multi-threaded concurrency control and resource pool management.', 'A Mutex has strict ownership (only the thread that acquired the lock can release it). A Counting Semaphore maintains a counter of available resources and acts as a signaling mechanism.', '["A Semaphore can only ever allow 1 thread to access a resource at all times, identical to a mutex","A Mutex allows any thread in the process to unlock it regardless of which thread acquired it","Counting Semaphores can only be used in single-threaded applications","A Mutex provides exclusive 1-thread locking with ownership; a Counting Semaphore tracks N available resource permits"]'::jsonb, 3)
